// Reactivity S4 — disposal + GC interaction under churn. A persistent watcher tracks `n`. Alongside it
// a loop creates and immediately disposes 50 transient effects, each of which subscribes to `n` on its
// one creation run. Disposal must sever every one of those subscriptions (and free the slot for reuse),
// so the later `n.set(1)` reruns only the *one* live watcher — not 51 — proving no disposed effect
// lingers on `n`'s subscriber list. The leak oracle separately asserts residency 0 at exit across all
// this create/dispose churn, and the differential asserts both backends churn byte-identically.
// expect: stdout "watch n=0"
// expect: stdout "watch n=1"
// expect: stdout "done"
// expect: exit 0
use std.reactive.{signal, effect}
n = signal(0)
effect(fn() {
    echo "watch n=${n.get()}"
})
mut i = 0
while i < 50 {
    tmp = effect(fn() { n.get() })
    tmp.dispose()
    i = i + 1
}
n.set(1)
echo "done"
